so right now json is the internet's chosen representation of metadata, and it's actually pretty good. douglas crockford got that one right. it's inflexible. having no trailing commas was a *great* decision. something something basin. but now there is all sorts of weird stuff happening on the internet. crawly spidey bots and ai scrapers and things of that sort. have you heard of web4? the information going on the internet just isn't *structured* for json anymore. 

so how do you denote data if you have a continuous bitstream, instead of a one with a pre-determined size, like a file? how do you denote where one field ends, and where one begins? can you denote more complicated datastreams, like repeating structures or nested structures? 

you could define all your [delimiters](/netdoc/2H) at the start of transmission. but this doesn't really get around the problem. it would require a top-level definition to determine how long the section for the *other* definitions is, basically necessitating a protocol for that one header. are the data chunks fixed length or variable length? either way, that has to go in the header too. if they're fixed, you need to denote how long each chunk is, and how many chunks there are. that should take up a few *words*, which is enough to contain that as well as a version number and a "magic number" identifier. if they're variable, you will also need to define... a delimiter for your delimiter... in your header chunk. and you *also* need to make sure that your delimiter for arbitrary length data *isn't included in any of the arbitrary data you are encoding*. turtles all the way down. 

let's build our way up with an example that is a marginal adaptation (or "improvement", if you like making things for the sake of making them) of one of the most common data formats in the world right now, jpeg. jpeg is an example of *even-length chunked data*. it uses a gradient for 8*8 pixel chunks, and these are the same chunks across every jpeg image. every jpeg interpreter in the world can rely on the jpeg encoding for the different chunks being the exact same so they can reconstruct the image. but here, we are going to explore *variable content*, *even-length chunked data*. 

the jpeg chunks were chosen because they are (close enough to) the most common things that the human eye sees. but there are cases where one might want to use a different set of chunks. if someone had a lot of images sourced from the same subject, say, foliage, they might want chunks that better represent foliage. so if you had a data set of these, and you determined your chunks, how would you transmit these images? for transmission savings, it makes sense to send chunks and images all at once. define all your chunks one time, 64 bits each, and then send all your images encoded with that representation. here it is advantageous if the size in bits of chunk defs plus image reps is less than however much it would have been to transmit these images with standard jpeg space. 